  "text_md": "PERSPECTIVE. A refecting-glass. PERSPICIL. An optic-glass. It occurs in Albumazar, 1634, sig. B.iv.\n\nPERSUADE . Persuasion.\n\nPERSTAND. To understand. Peele.\n\nPERSUADERS. Spurs . pistols. Also;\n\nFor hete her clothes down sche dede\n\nPERS WAY. To mitigate. Ben Jonson, iv. 428. PERT. Beautifully  ` delicate: It is the translation of subtilis in Gesta Rom. p. 142.\n\nAlmest to her gerdyl stede,\n\nSche was whyt as lylye yn May,\n\nThan lay sche uncovert ;\n\nOr snow that sneweth yn wynterys day,\n\nIllustrations of Fairy Mythology , P. 1l.\n\nHe seygh never non sO pert.\n\nPERTE: (1) To part. Still in use. Then Thomas a sory man was The terys ran out of his een gray Lufy lady, 3et tell thou me If we shalle perte for ever and ay. he,\n\n(2) Of good appearance. Ther was no man in the kynges lande More perte then was he.\n\nPERTELICHE. Openly. A-N)\n\nPresez hls penowne, and pertly ỉt hentes.\n\nPERTENERE. A partner.\n\nMorte Arthure , MS. Lincoln , f.84. PERTELOTE. The name of a hen.\n\nGod graunt u8 mekenesse in angurs here, And grace to lede owre lyfe here 800,\n\nOf hevene, whan we hens schall goo.\n\nThat may aftur be pertenere\n\n## PERTRYCHE. A partridge.\n\nclaues and nayles of the hauke, is as halfe deed for drede. Carton', Divers Fruytful Ghostly Maters.\n\nPERTY.\n\nPERTURBE To trouble. Palsgrave.\n\nGyffe thaym grace wel to the, That lystyns me whyle ;\n\nPart. Lydgate. God that sittls 1n Trlnité,\n\nAlle that lovys of melody,\n\nTheyrr soules shelde fro peryle:\n\nOffhevon bllsse God graunte tham perty,\n\nMS:\n\nPERUSE. To examine, or survey.\n\n## PERUR A kind of cup.\n\nMonsleur Soublez haperused the feet, returned to the king, and told hlm there was pothing ready and that the mariners and Jot to goe the voyage till they were paid ther arrears. ving yeeld\n\nPERVINKE. The herb periwinkle. (A-s)\n\nPERVEY To provide. (A-N)\n\nPERYE.\n\nBeforc the towre undur a peryo.\n\npear-tree. (A.-N) But for hur lorde sche durste not done, That sate benethe and pleyed hym merye,\n\n## PERYSSE. Pears. (A.-N)\n\nAnd began down to falle.\n\nA gorget of mail or plate attached to the helmet. A pesane and a paunsone; MS. Morte Arthure, f. 89.\n\nPESATE. Is wben a managed horse rises handsomely before and upon his haunches, and av the same time bendshis fore-legs up tohisbody. PESE. (1) Peace. Perceval 980, 981.\n\nTylle y be sewre of youre hartys ese,\n\nPESEN. Peas. This is the common early form of word, and occurs in\\_Chaucer, Legende of Good Women, 648. Holloway gives the following couplet, as seen lately ou a board in pea-field in Berkshire the\n\nShut the Because the plgs shouldn't get Into the peason. gate\n\nin his 133d epigram.\n\nThen acute sorrell , and sweet three-leav'd grasse, And for 2 sawce he seldome is at charges, For every crab-tree doth afoord him vergls His banket sometImee is greene beanes and peason , Nuts, peares, plumbes, apples, as they are in season .\n\nAs for his sallets, better never was\n\nPESIBLE. Peaceable. (A.-N) PESIBLETE. A calm. A-N)\n\nPESON An instrument in the form of a staff; with balls or crockets, used for weighing before scales were employed.\n\nPESK. A peach. Nominale MS-\n\nPESS. A hassock. Suffolk.\n\nPESSIPE. A kind of cup.\n\nPESSCOD-SCALDING. kind of merry making in summer evenings the treat, green field peas boiled in the shells. Yorksh.\n\nPESTERED. Crowded. Peele, ii. 235.\n\nPESTLE: (1) A leg of an animal, generally of a pig: A pestle of pork is still in common use. Pestels   of venison; P. 98. Pestell of fesshe; jambon; A pestle-pie is a standing pie which con tains a whole gammon, and sometimes a couple at country fairs, and at Christmas fcasts. large\n\nPESTERMENT . Embarrassment. North.\n\n(2) A constable's staff.\n\nPETE. See Cov. Myst.\n\nPÉSTLE-HEAD. blockhead\n\nLong lay the kyng,there away wolde not hee Dayly he propherld batayle : the enmys durst not fyghte Lacke of logynge and vltayle It was grett peté,\n\nLowe, how the good Lorde hls owne gentill kuyzle,\n\nCausid the gentill prynce to remeve, siche was Goddes myzte\n\nBecause he shulde rememblr hym In wele and In woo, Thus in every thyng, Lorde, thy wIlle be doo Reg\n\nMany\n\nPETEOSE. Merciful compassionate.\n\nOff moders and of thelr gret dcsolatioun,\n\nmen\n\nspekes of lamentacioun,\n\nWhich that thay did indure When that thelr chílder dy and passe, But of hls peteose tender moder, alasre am verray sure,\n\nThe wo and payn passis alle othere.",
